docs/current-language.md:36:| Execution | Native code is the only runtime: the compiler lowers to Core IR, emits LLVM and links with the C runtime. Builds are debug by default (one LLVM unit per module); `--release` optimizes the whole program as one unit. `mylang build` writes an executable; `mylang run`, `run-project` and `test` build one and execute it in a child process; `mylang dump-ir` prints the Core IR | [native builds](tooling/native-build.md), [performance](performance.md#build-for-speed); [direct C imports](c-ffi.md) and [guest tests](tooling/guest-tests.md) describe the native route |
SPEC.md:1383:- [NEW-CORE-S5-CLI-1] `mylang build` (and `build --run`), `run`, `run-project` and `test` run the new core (`src/core/cli.zig`), and `dump-ir`, `dump-typed` and `coverage` are `mylang` developer commands. They take the old CLI's spellings: `--flag VALUE` or `--flag=VALUE`; `--qbe PATH` names a tool the new core never runs and is ignored; `--project` and `--whole-program` ask for nothing more (S5-D6); `--mode ReleaseFast` is `--release`. A build or run prints the front end's warnings to stderr before the program runs, as the old CLI did, and `--diagnostics=json` renders them, check errors, the new core's refusals and a trap as `diagnostic-v1` records (a trap's record carries its frames and the runtime's `trap_detail`).
